# Plain language for user-facing text

When writing text shown to end users (UI labels, errors, footers, tooltips, methodology pages, help copy, empty states, marketing copy, exported JSON messages):

## No code or terminal instructions

- Do not include shell commands, Python/terminal snippets, file paths, package names, or `<code>` / backtick formatting for technical instructions.
- Do not tell users to run scripts, npm commands, or edit config files in the product UI.
- Use plain language instead (e.g. "Please refresh the page" instead of "run python -m ...").
- Developer/setup instructions belong only in README or internal docs, not in dashboard or app surfaces.

## No em dashes or en dashes

- Do not use em dashes (—) or en dashes (–) in any user-facing text.
- Use commas, periods, colons, or the word "to" for ranges instead (e.g. "$15,001 to $50,000", not "$15,001–$50,000").
- Apply the same rule to assistant responses, commit messages, and PR descriptions when writing for humans.

Applies to React components, Python export strings shown in the UI, and any copy a non-technical reader would see.
